1
00:00:05,924 --> 00:00:08,320
Hello, I'm Sangsoo Jeong.

2
00:00:08,321 --> 00:00:12,800
This time, let's talk about various tips for when you draw.

3
00:00:14,110 --> 00:00:16,079
Actually, they're nothing grand.

4
00:00:16,081 --> 00:00:23,680
I'll just share short tips about things I couldn't cover while teaching.

5
00:00:23,681 --> 00:00:27,440
I always told you to save often while drawing.

6
00:00:28,455 --> 00:00:33,600
Not just overwriting the same file, but saving the drawing at different times.

7
00:00:33,680 --> 00:00:36,720
I save every one or two hours.

8
00:00:36,721 --> 00:00:39,840
That way, the intermediate process of my drawing remains.

9
00:00:39,841 --> 00:00:43,321
It's also good when I look back and analyze it after finishing a piece.

10
00:00:43,405 --> 00:00:47,560
Or I can post the process on my social media later to show what I've achieved.

11
00:00:47,561 --> 00:00:53,361
If people are curious about my work process, you could say I'm a decently successful painter.

12
00:00:54,390 --> 00:00:57,640
What I'll talk about today is a slightly different subject.

13
00:00:57,641 --> 00:01:02,600
Through the work process left behind like this, you can make your drawing even better.

14
00:01:08,239 --> 00:01:11,520
Mechanical analog watches, people don't really use them these days.

15
00:01:11,521 --> 00:01:18,080
They have value as a metal object, but in terms of accuracy, they can't hold a candle to digital watches.

16
00:01:18,080 --> 00:01:26,480
No matter how precisely a mechanical watch is made, over a long time errors accumulate and the time becomes inaccurate.

17
00:01:26,481 --> 00:01:35,840
The reason I bring this up is that even if you think you're drawing well, as time goes by your drawing changes, for better or worse.

18
00:01:36,560 --> 00:01:37,520
I said this before.

19
00:01:37,520 --> 00:01:43,840
If you keep looking at your drawing, you forget whether you're going in the right direction.

20
00:01:43,841 --> 00:01:51,881
And if you have a comparison target, like the drawing from a few hours ago, you can reduce the errors that creep in over time.

21
00:01:52,669 --> 00:01:58,960
If you compare like this and think the current drawing is clearly better, just keep going.

22
00:01:58,961 --> 00:02:04,400
But if you think the past drawing is better, of course you should go back to the past.

23
00:02:04,401 --> 00:02:12,240
If you do it this way, on the assumption that you have a discerning eye, your drawing will have no choice but to go in a good direction.

24
00:02:12,241 --> 00:02:14,880
And you develop that discerning eye through lots of appreciation.

25
00:02:16,595 --> 00:02:22,540
When you compare the old and the current and can't tell which one is better, what do you do?

26
00:02:22,541 --> 00:02:23,541
Especially with faces.

27
00:02:24,384 --> 00:02:29,740
In that case, there's a simple method. I combine the past with the present.

28
00:02:30,960 --> 00:02:35,520
If you do that, in most cases a more plausible drawing will come out.

29
00:02:35,521 --> 00:02:38,080
Let me tell you why.

30
00:02:38,081 --> 00:02:48,320
When you do a rough sketch, you like the drawing, but the more you make it concrete, the worse the feeling gets compared to the rough sketch. You've probably experienced this a lot.

31
00:02:48,321 --> 00:02:51,280
I've experienced it a lot too.

32
00:02:51,281 --> 00:02:58,921
That's because, in the process of correcting the uncertainty that appears in a rough sketch in your head, you unconsciously imagine it in a better direction.

33
00:02:59,810 --> 00:03:04,740
It's like looking at a foggy bathroom mirror and thinking, "Oh, that's not bad."

34
00:03:05,890 --> 00:03:14,180
If you overlap the past drawing and the current drawing by adjusting the opacity, the uncertainty reappears.

35
00:03:14,181 --> 00:03:21,220
We use our imagination again, and our drawing gets the chance and strength to go in a better direction.

36
00:03:22,750 --> 00:03:30,880
If you've seen posts about an idol group's average face, or the average Korean face, you probably found them better looking and prettier than expected.

37
00:03:30,885 --> 00:03:32,640
Why is that?

38
00:03:32,641 --> 00:03:38,480
Just search the internet for "average face" right now, and so many attractive faces come up.

39
00:03:38,481 --> 00:03:40,960
You think, "This is the average."

40
00:03:41,499 --> 00:03:44,560
They're surprisingly so attractive.

41
00:03:44,561 --> 00:04:01,540
Psychologists discovered through experiments that people found faces more attractive the more they were averaged: four faces rather than two, eight rather than four, sixteen rather than eight, and so on.

42
00:04:01,541 --> 00:04:07,060
In the first chapter, I told you that ordinariness can become a weapon.

43
00:04:07,061 --> 00:04:18,019
The more a person's face is combined in different ways, the closer it gets to the average, and an average face is familiar to us, and familiarity means likability.

44
00:04:18,020 --> 00:04:21,380
And depending on that likability, we feel even greater attraction.

45
00:04:22,780 --> 00:04:24,350
Shall we try it as an example?

46
00:04:24,351 --> 00:04:29,950
Looking at this drawing now, the version I drew later seems to have a better face.

47
00:04:29,951 --> 00:04:31,630
So let me try compositing them.

48
00:04:47,935 --> 00:04:50,090
After compositing, how is it?

49
00:04:50,170 --> 00:04:55,450
It got a little blurry, but I think the feeling got better.

50
00:04:55,451 --> 00:04:59,091
Here I should revive the sharpness through retouching and refine it.

51
00:04:59,560 --> 00:05:03,491
Of course, after retouching, I need to compare this version again.

52
00:05:03,750 --> 00:05:05,850
Keep repeating until I'm satisfied.

53
00:05:07,375 --> 00:05:10,801
You can also use it when doing concept art.

54
00:05:11,005 --> 00:05:13,720
Let me composite this drawing here.

55
00:05:16,175 --> 00:05:20,320
I took it out like this and attached it to match the positions of the eyes and nose.

56
00:05:21,740 --> 00:05:28,581
Then I lower the opacity and adjust the value with a curve.

57
00:05:30,840 --> 00:05:33,610
And I match the color too.

58
00:05:47,135 --> 00:05:48,135
How is it?

59
00:05:50,575 --> 00:05:54,250
With a new comparison target like this, there's also a fun of picking and choosing.

60
00:05:55,425 --> 00:06:02,060
The more drawings you make, the more face references you've drawn, so there'll be more material to composite.

61
00:06:02,061 --> 00:06:05,100
But you must not composite other people's drawings or photos.

62
00:06:05,101 --> 00:06:06,101
Absolutely not.

63
00:06:07,160 --> 00:06:13,171
However, even if it's your own drawing, don't just take it from somewhere else and composite it.

64
00:06:14,680 --> 00:06:19,091
Only use it when you composite a past version of the same drawing.

65
00:06:23,990 --> 00:06:28,920
I said that when you draw, you need to bring out the sense of volume well.

66
00:06:28,921 --> 00:06:31,320
It's no different when drawing weapons.

67
00:06:31,321 --> 00:06:34,040
Weapons are usually drawn facing forward like this.

68
00:06:34,041 --> 00:06:39,160
It's easy to draw, and it's efficient for making sheets for 3D work.

69
00:06:39,161 --> 00:06:45,080
However, in a drawing, a weapon facing forward like this becomes a factor that lowers the sense of volume.

70
00:06:45,081 --> 00:06:48,680
You should avoid it as much as possible in illustrations.

71
00:06:48,681 --> 00:06:52,360
There's no need to completely avoid it, but first design it facing forward and draw it.

72
00:06:53,125 --> 00:06:59,280
Then make a few modifications, turn the angle, apply it, and arrange it.

73
00:06:59,281 --> 00:07:00,281
Shall we do it?

74
00:07:02,390 --> 00:07:05,480
Let me rotate this weapon a little like this.

75
00:07:05,481 --> 00:07:06,840
Turn on free transform.

76
00:07:09,000 --> 00:07:17,920
Use warp to give it a slightly turned feel.

77
00:07:22,890 --> 00:07:29,130
But using it like this as-is is a bit awkward, right?

78
00:07:29,131 --> 00:07:30,570
Grasp the volume well.

79
00:07:43,890 --> 00:07:44,940
Cut out the needed parts.

80
00:07:44,941 --> 00:07:48,501
Since it's turned like this, this side will be more visible, right?

81
00:07:51,770 --> 00:07:55,980
Move it so this side is more visible.

82
00:07:55,981 --> 00:07:58,300
This side will be barely visible.

83
00:07:58,301 --> 00:08:00,540
Move it so it's just barely visible like this.

84
00:08:05,110 --> 00:08:08,040
Fill it in with free transform.

85
00:08:29,780 --> 00:08:30,830
You can also use the warp tool.

86
00:08:30,831 --> 00:08:34,270
And this side will also be seen very small, right?

87
00:08:35,799 --> 00:08:37,270
I'll fix this side too.

88
00:09:11,350 --> 00:09:13,750
I'll fix this side as well.

89
00:09:28,079 --> 00:09:34,430
The work process itself isn't hard, but grasping the volume well is important.

90
00:09:39,420 --> 00:09:41,950
Let me do this part the same way.

91
00:10:07,390 --> 00:10:08,390
If I do that.

92
00:10:37,820 --> 00:10:40,270
If you do it like this, you can easily rotate it.

93
00:10:41,960 --> 00:10:44,600
If you compare, it changed like this.

94
00:10:44,601 --> 00:10:51,480
If you fit this to the character now, you can give a sense of volume to the drawing effectively without spending much time.

95
00:10:56,630 --> 00:11:03,480
The method of saving a sketch drawn on the background layer is a very easy method that isn't hard at all.

96
00:11:04,125 --> 00:11:09,720
There's a method commonly known as masking, but there's an even easier method.

97
00:11:09,800 --> 00:11:14,040
Click the Channels panel.

98
00:11:14,041 --> 00:11:19,880
Hold Control and click the RGB channel preview.

99
00:11:22,539 --> 00:11:27,861
In this state, load the selection, then create a layer.

100
00:11:29,345 --> 00:11:30,940
Then just fill in the paint.

101
00:11:35,720 --> 00:11:36,720
It's been separated.

102
00:11:47,510 --> 00:11:52,520
Many people worry about how to handle the line of sight when drawing.

103
00:11:52,521 --> 00:11:57,880
There's no right answer, but I'll show you a way to draw it without being awkward.

104
00:11:57,881 --> 00:12:01,480
The pupils can move freely at my will.

105
00:12:01,481 --> 00:12:07,441
If you want to look to the right, you move to the right; if you want to look to the left, you move to the left.

106
00:12:07,565 --> 00:12:08,920
That's obvious.

107
00:12:08,921 --> 00:12:14,040
Then I just need to roll only the pupils in the direction I want to look, right?

108
00:12:14,041 --> 00:12:15,041
No.

109
00:12:15,535 --> 00:12:20,280
If you only roll your eyes, we call that a "side-eye," meaning rolling only the eyes.

110
00:12:20,281 --> 00:12:25,400
In general, when we look somewhere, our head also moves.

111
00:12:25,401 --> 00:12:29,080
You need to express that in the drawing too.

112
00:12:29,081 --> 00:12:35,441
When expressing a normal line of sight, it's good to naturally move the head along with the gaze.

113
00:12:36,139 --> 00:12:40,700
At this point, if the eyes are fixed and only the head turns, it's very awkward.

114
00:12:41,985 --> 00:12:46,700
Moving only the pupils is possible in specific situations.

115
00:12:46,750 --> 00:12:50,940
When you need emotions like primness or slyness, that is.

116
00:12:50,941 --> 00:12:55,420
In a drawing that doesn't need such emotions, you shouldn't move only the pupils.

117
00:12:56,920 --> 00:12:58,810
Let me confirm with the drawing.

118
00:13:00,519 --> 00:13:06,090
You can see that the head slightly turns along with the direction of the gaze like this.

119
00:13:11,710 --> 00:13:17,600
To give this character a prim aloofness, I didn't turn the head much.

120
00:13:19,800 --> 00:13:26,531
To express this character's slyness, I likewise didn't turn the head much.

121
00:13:29,475 --> 00:13:30,990
The eyes also deliberately try to look aloof.

122
00:13:33,875 --> 00:13:39,431
This drawing is likewise to express a slight primness, so I didn't turn the head much.

123
00:13:41,020 --> 00:13:44,750
In this drawing, the head naturally turns along with the gaze.

124
00:13:47,555 --> 00:13:53,310
Since your drawing can become clumsy from such small things, you need to draw carefully.

125
00:14:00,005 --> 00:14:05,760
Other than this, I wanted to talk about various tips, but they're all on the internet.

126
00:14:05,761 --> 00:14:09,200
So I'll show you a demonstration of drawing the back view and wrap up.

127
00:14:12,925 --> 00:14:16,120
As usual, I start with a sketch.

128
00:14:16,121 --> 00:14:20,120
When drawing the back view, especially, you need to make sure the design is clearly visible.

129
00:14:21,370 --> 00:14:25,740
So to make the sleeve edge visible, I stretched the arm out.

130
00:14:27,070 --> 00:14:29,760
But actually, I didn't draw the sleeve edge.

131
00:14:29,761 --> 00:14:34,200
Since it's not for sheet work, I only drew the body.

132
00:14:34,945 --> 00:14:38,741
Following the simplified sketch method, I draw the body roughly.

133
00:14:40,310 --> 00:14:44,480
For the sketch, I only do the minimal rendering needed.

134
00:14:48,945 --> 00:14:56,211
When designing the back view, just think of it as naturally extending the front view and draw.

135
00:14:57,639 --> 00:15:02,600
If you want to give it a twist of charm where the front and back differ, I really wouldn't stop you.

136
00:15:03,770 --> 00:15:13,580
If you think that's effective, that is.

137
00:15:13,581 --> 00:15:16,540
I keep sketching along.

138
00:15:16,541 --> 00:15:22,780
Personally, since I don't have to draw the face, I feel less burdened drawing the back view.

139
00:15:22,781 --> 00:15:24,940
And there's already a design here.

140
00:15:24,941 --> 00:15:30,300
Usually when doing sheet work, I finish the back view work in about an hour or two.

141
00:16:09,315 --> 00:16:13,470
This time, I'll do it the way I normally work.

142
00:16:13,471 --> 00:16:15,790
I won't use many layers.

143
00:16:15,791 --> 00:16:17,150
I lay down the base color.

144
00:16:34,675 --> 00:16:50,060
I'll do all the coloring on one layer.

145
00:16:50,061 --> 00:16:52,460
I'll only do simple value rendering too.

146
00:17:26,784 --> 00:17:29,580
But I shouldn't draw the hair.

147
00:17:29,581 --> 00:17:32,700
Because the back design needs to be visible.

148
00:17:32,701 --> 00:17:36,940
If the hair shape has a special design, I should draw it separately on the side.

149
00:17:45,999 --> 00:17:50,041
Now let me merge the layers and proceed.

150
00:18:01,794 --> 00:18:07,310
When I do sheet work, I draw while keeping the line as much as possible.

151
00:18:07,311 --> 00:18:10,510
That way the design shows better.

152
00:18:10,510 --> 00:18:11,710
And it takes less time.

153
00:18:15,759 --> 00:18:18,000
From here on, I'll just keep rendering.

154
00:18:19,309 --> 00:18:23,161
Since I already explained all of this, there's not much to explain.

155
00:18:24,465 --> 00:18:29,290
Think of it as a blueprint that shows the design, and paint briefly without straining.

156
00:19:12,604 --> 00:19:17,160
Through this chapter and up to now, I've shared my know-how through various demonstrations.

157
00:19:18,749 --> 00:19:22,720
I tried to share with you as much as I knew. How was it?

158
00:19:27,134 --> 00:19:33,130
It was a class for intermediate students, but I think I mainly tried to share what I wanted to.

159
00:19:33,131 --> 00:19:39,250
The content might be hard for some and already familiar for others.

160
00:19:41,044 --> 00:19:46,430
For those who found it hard, I'd say I've created goals for you to learn going forward.

161
00:19:48,279 --> 00:19:54,291
For those who already know, I hope it gave you the confidence that drawing isn't such a big deal.

162
00:19:55,674 --> 00:19:59,880
Actually, I think even a five-year-old has things you can learn from.

163
00:20:01,179 --> 00:20:06,510
There will be a difference in degree, but I don't think there's nothing to learn from this class.

164
00:20:07,769 --> 00:20:12,180
I just hope you got your money's worth for your precious cost.

165
00:20:15,874 --> 00:20:31,470
I wonder if I did too much, but whatever, it's already been filmed. If I make a new class later, please buy it again too. I'll study hard and think about whether there's anything more I can share. Thank you, everyone.

166
00:20:38,360 --> 00:20:43,171
This drawing is going to be cut off abruptly midway.

167
00:20:43,719 --> 00:20:44,970
Let me say goodbye in advance.

168
00:20:47,769 --> 00:20:56,380
In the next chapter, we'll have a talk about mindset, like mind maps, and a Q&A session.

169
00:20:56,381 --> 00:20:57,381
Good work, everyone.
